  "levels": {
    "1": {
      "title": "California says no to a tax on billionaires",
      "text": "People in California voted on a tax for very rich people. The answer was no. The tax was 5% of their wealth. It was for people with more than $1.1bn. It wanted to raise about $100bn for health care. Many leaders were against it, and they won."
    },
    "2": {
      "title": "California voters reject a tax on billionaires",
      "text": "Voters in California have rejected a one-time wealth tax on billionaires. It took almost a week to count the votes after the midterm election. The plan, called Proposition 40, would have charged 5% of the total wealth of people with more than $1.1bn. About 200 billionaires live in the state.\n\nThe tax would have raised about $100bn for health care, to replace money lost after federal cuts. It failed after Democrats and unions disagreed. Governor Gavin Newsom and Google co-founder Sergey Brin were against it. Newsom feared that rich people would leave the state and take their income tax with them. The head of the union behind the plan, Dave Regan, said it will try again in 2028."
    },
    "3": {
      "title": "Californians reject wealth tax for billionaires",
      "text": "California voters have rejected a proposed wealth tax on the state's billionaires. It took nearly a week of counting after the midterm election to project the result. Proposition 40 would have imposed a one-time 5% tax on the total wealth, including shares, of people worth more than $1.1bn. About 200 billionaires live in California.\n\nThe measure was meant to raise about $100bn to make up for federal healthcare funding cuts linked to President Donald Trump's tax and spending bill. Supporters argued the ultra-wealthy should pay their fair share. It failed amid divisions among Democrats and labour unions.\n\nGovernor Gavin Newsom opposed the plan, saying it could push the wealthy to leave and reduce income tax revenue. Google co-founder Sergey Brin also opposed it, and according to the Los Angeles Times he gave more than $100m to a group funding ballot measures that could block the tax. Dave Regan, who leads the union that backed the proposal, said the opposition caused confusion and promised to push for a billionaire tax again in 2028."
    }
  },
